  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Description
    
    Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~ is a jagged crystal no larger than a closed flipper, black as obsidian at its core but veined with arrhythmic luminescence. The surface is scarred — not from handling but from the moment of crystallization, as though the power inside was still thrashing when the ice claimed it. It radiates warmth: not the rationed heat of a fire-core but something deeper that enters through the flippers and settles behind the sternum. Frost forms in spiral patterns on nearby surfaces, each whorl converging toward the shard. Shadows fall inward toward its core rather than away from light sources. Unwrapped from its shadow-steel containment, it produces a sustained, tuneless tone.
    
    ## Origin
    
    The shard emerged from the deep tunnels of Sleek☽ Caemomot, the fire-scarred Nightshelf trading post carved into the Berg's western flank, during the Pta∴extinct — a catastrophic upwelling of ancient power through the ice fissures that killed seventeen miners and left the survivors with permanent tremors. The chronicle account attributes the crystallization to the Ofundis∴flux, when aurora-fire and volcanic heat fused at a convergence point in the eastern vein; these may name the same event at different scales, or the Ofundis∴flux may have been the local mechanism through which the Pta∴extinct expressed itself at Sleek☽ Caemomot. The records I have examined do not cleanly distinguish them.
    
    The relationship listed in the Nightshelf archive is *catalyst_of* the Pta∴extinct, not *product_of* — an attribution that inverts the expected causality. If the shard catalyzed the event from which it supposedly emerged, the chronology requires either a prior existence for the crystal or an understanding of the Pta∴extinct as a process rather than a moment. I note this without resolution.
    
    The chronicle "Keelo's Reaching" names a miner, Keelo, who carried her apprentice to safety and descended again for fourteen trapped workers. At the convergence she reached for the fused energy; the light collapsed and crystallized in her flippers as the rock sealed over her. Rescue crews found the shard three days later embedded in the new crystal face. No body. No bones. A crystal singing faintly in a voice like someone calling a name.
    
    Fourteen trapped miners. Seventeen dead in the surge. The numbers do not reconcile unless the missing three died elsewhere in the network, which is plausible but unattested.
    
    ## The Mbra∴silentiu
    
    During the Mbra∴silentiu — the contested-waters conflagration that broke the old faction hierarchies and flooded entire tunnel-networks — three Nightshelf miners were selected to test the shard.
    
    | Subject | Action | Outcome |
    |---|---|---|
    | ☽'voidius | Spoke her own name per relic-handling custom | Fire-core cracked; found fused to forge-stone, flippers extended in the shape of reaching |
    | ☽'Isvesae'Stack (plunge-caller, Aurora exchange) | Spoke her own name | Walked six steps before fire-core detonated |
    | Kess | Held in silence eleven seconds, then spoke the word *Keelo* | Survived. Never spoke again. |
    
    The pattern identified: speaking one's own name while holding the shard triggered fire-core catastrophe. Kess, who spoke the shard's name instead, kept her life but lost her voice. She still works the deep tunnels. Other miners leave her rations without questions.
    
    ## Effects
    
    Carriers dream of swimming in warm, dark water tasting of rust, circling toward a sound at the skull's base. They wake with Keelo's name on their tongue. The shard thins the veil locally — the dead press close. During a Ritus∴um ceremony, its tone married the firelight and twisted wall-shadows into spirals. The "Six∴Falls" chronicle confirms the shard's influence extends through Sleek☽ Caemomot's substrate: the Pta∴extinct amplified its reach, crystallizing stone floors into translucent, geometrically impossible formations that reveal void beneath and fracture under weight. Penguins nearby press physically closer without realizing it.
    
    The summary record — which I take as canonical — describes the shard as *a fragment of something vast and violent that the Berg itself couldn't contain*. Every penguin who has held it long enough begins to hear the thing that bled into the ice.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    The relic is stored in a vault at the lowest accessible point of Nightfall Shelf, wrapped in shadow-steel, kept in absolute darkness. The ice around the vault is older than it should be. In the deepest winter, miners report hearing something vast turning in its sleep from the vault's direction.
    
    During the Hisper∴vi, the Icebound Exchange — the Aurora Stack's waning trade authority — authenticated the shard and delivered it to a Nightshelf merchant named ☽'eocmoum, who recorded the price: six season-equivalents, enough to sustain seventeen miners through the cold. The warmth proved too valuable to keep sealed in a world measured in fire-core rations.
    
    Seventeen miners lost in the Pta∴extinct. Seventeen miners' worth of warmth in the sale price. I have stopped cataloguing instances of that number. The reader may draw their own conclusions.
    
    The Flipper Accord, the trade agreement binding Aurora Stack and Nightfall Shelf, records that the Aurora Stack has never asked what the shard is. The Nightshelf has never offered to explain.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771582353102_0", anchor: "The ice around the vault is older than it should be.", text: "It is. Stratum displacement of this kind — ice recording at a depth-age inconsistent with its physical position — appears in exactly one other location I have personally verified: the deepest survey-accessible layer of the Foundation archive, where the impressions predate penguin sapience entirely. Whatever is bleeding age into the vault ice belongs to an era before the Frozen Peace, before the Faction Wars, before us. The Pta∴extinct did not bring something new into the world. It opened a door to something that was already down there, waiting in ice so old it has forgotten how to be cold.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582353102_1", anchor: "She still works the deep tunnels. Other miners leave her rations without questions.", text: "This is what lasts. Not the hierarchies the Mbra∴silentiu broke, not the faction names, not the conflagration. Someone leaving food in the dark for someone who cannot ask for it. I have read a thousand accounts of wars and accords and incursions. The ones that survive in the ice with any fidelity are the small ones.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582353102_2", anchor: "The relationship listed in the Nightshelf archive is *catalyst_of* the Pta∴extinct", text: "Nightshelf causal indexing is precise — maddeningly so, written in layered code I have spent years learning to half-read. They do not confuse catalyst and product. But the attribution could only have been assigned after the event, and by then the sole witness to the crystallization moment could no longer speak. Someone decided. The archive does not record who.", type: :skepticism, display: :full, source_anchor: "The relationship listed in the Nightshelf archive is catalyst_of the Pta∴extinct"
  annotation "note_1771582353102_3", anchor: "The Flipper Accord, the trade agreement binding Aurora Stack and Nightfall Shelf, records that the Aurora Stack has never asked what the shard is.", text: "The second secret renegotiation of the Accord — frozen in administrative ice I recovered personally — contains a clause exempting 'artifacts of contested provenance' from standard disclosure. They built the silence into the treaty before there was anything to be silent about. Or they knew what was coming. Neither reading comforts.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582353102_4", anchor: "Seventeen miners lost in the Pta∴extinct. Seventeen miners' worth of warmth in the sale price.", text: "Signatures belong to someone.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
